```
In the test method, `PexMethod.Replay` will invoke all PEX-generated test methods, providing you the ability to test all conditions under which `IndexOf`, among others, can return a negative result or a non-negative result. Note that if you have other test cases in your `.cs` file, PEX will only pick these if they are the only ones in your `.cs` file.

Also, it's crucial to have suitable exception test cases. For example, in C#, if you are checking for an index that is lower than zero, i.e., `IndexOutOfRangeException`, you must have corresponding test cases. 

#### Contracts
Contract testing is a type of testing that verifies that the contract provided by a certain software module (or subsystem) is upheld by the module during execution. These contracts are specifications for how this module should behave. To carry out this testing, the system needs to follow all the rules and constraints defined.

In the context of a contract, a testable condition is a predicate that relates the outcome of an action to some properties (e.g., preconditions) and the outcome (e.g., postconditions). A contract in the context of this guide refers to the predicates involved in an operation or an action.

#### Precondition and Postcondition
In the context of a method, the precondition is the set of conditions that need to be true before you can call a method. The postcondition is a state that is guaranteed after your method has been called. It involves the parameters and return values of the method.

#### Replay Attribute
Pex can execute contracts in the generated PEX-generated test methods. You use the `PexMethod.Replay` method to call a PEX-generated method. This is a static method called `Replay()` that can be used to execute PEX-generated test methods so the full range of PEX-generated test methods can be executed.

#### Observe Attribute
You can use the `PexObserve.ValueForViewing` attribute (which is only an indication rather than a requirement) to log values such as function parameters, return values, list sizes, data content, etc., to the output window for easy reference during debugging. The `PexObserve.ValueForViewing` attribute is useful when you would like to inspect values of variables at different points of time.--------------------            
